entic AI: RAG vs. Fine-Tuning"], "prefix": "10.1007", "author": [{"given": "Sumit", "family": "Ranjan", "sequence": "first", "affiliation": [], "role": [{"vocabulary": "crossref", "role": "author"}]}, {"given": "Divya", "family": "Chembachere", "sequence": "additional", "affiliation": [], "role": [{"vocabulary": "crossref", "role": "author"}]}, {"given": "Lanwin", "family": "Lobo", "sequence": "additional", "affiliation": [], "role": [{"vocabulary": "crossref", "role": "author"}]}], "member": "297", "published-online": {"date-parts": [[2025, 8, 14]]}, "container-title": ["Agentic AI in Enterprise"], "language": "en", "link": [{"URL": "https://link.springer.com/content/pdf/10.1007/979-8-8688-1542-3_4", "content-type": "unspecified", "content-version": "vor", "intended-application": "similarity-checking"}], "deposited": {"date-parts": [[2026, 5, 16]], "date-time": "2026-05-16T00:21:15Z", "timestamp": 1778890875000}, "score": 24.926113, "resource": {"primary": {"URL": "https://link.springer.com/10.1007/979-8-8688-1542-3_4"}}, "issued": {"date-parts": [[2025]]}, "ISBN": ["9798868815416", "9798868815423"], "references-count": 0, "URL": "https://doi.org/10.1007/979-8-8688-1542-3_4", "published": {"date-parts": [[2025]]}, "assertion": [{"value": "14 August 2025", "order": 1, "name": "first_online", "label": "First Online", "group": {"name": "ChapterHistory", "label": "Chapter History"}}]}, {"indexed": {"date-parts": [[2026, 6, 12]], "date-time": "2026-06-12T16:06:03Z", "timestamp": 1781280363537, "version": "3.54.1"}, "posted": {"date-parts": [[2025, 9, 11]]}, "reference-count": 0, "publisher": "Institute of Electrical and Electronics Engineers (IEEE)", "license": [{"start": {"date-parts": [[2025, 9, 11]], "date-time": "2025-09-11T00:00:00Z", "timestamp": 1757548800000}, "content-version": "unspecified", "delay-in-days": 0, "URL": "https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/"}], "content-domain": {"domain": [], "crossmark-restriction": false}, "abstract": "<jats:p>Large language models have demonstrated remarkable capabilities in text generation and problem solving, yet they continue to face fundamental challenges such as hallucination, lack of factual grounding, and limited reasoning reliability. The core of these issues lies the question of how LLMs acquire and use knowledge. While internal knowledge embedded in model parameters enables impressive generalization, it is often insufficient for up-to-date or domain-specific tasks. External knowledge integration, such as retrieval-augmented generation (RAG), provides grounding and factuality but introduces challenges of retrieval quality, latency, and reliability. Beyond these paradigms, recent advances in agentic LLMs extend models from passive generators to active problem solvers that can reason, plan, and interact with external tools. This survey provides a unified, knowledge-centric perspective on LLMs, organized along three complementary dimensions: (i) reactive: internal knowledge, (ii) lightactive: external knowledge, and (iii) proactive: agentic knowledge utilization for reasoning and tool interaction. We provide a taxonomy of knowledge usage in LLMs, analyze their respective strengths and limitations, and highlight how these paradigms interact in real-world systems. Finally, we identify open challenges to facilitate future research.</jats:p>", "DOI": "10.36227/techrxiv.175756679.96515147/v1", "type": "posted-content", "created": {"date-parts": [[2025, 9, 11]], "date-time": "2025-09-11T05:00:02Z", "timestamp": 1757566802000}, "source": "Crossref", "is-referenced-by-count": 0, "title": ["Unifying Knowledge in Agentic LLMs: Concepts, Methods, and Recent Advancements"], "prefix": "10.36227", "author": [{"ORCID": "https://orcid.org/0000-0002-3758-4041", "authenticated-orcid": false, "given": "Lihui", "family": "Liu", "sequence": "first", "affiliation": [{"name": "Wayne State University"}], "role": [{"vocabulary": "crossref", "role": "author"}]}, {"given": "Kai", "family": "Shu", "sequence": "additional", "affiliation": [{"name": "Emory University"}], "role": [{"vocabulary": "crossref", "role": "author"}]}], "member": "263", "link": [{"URL": "https://www.techrxiv.org/doi/pdf/10.36227/techrxiv.175756679.96515147/v1", "content-type": "unspecified", "content-version": "vor", "intended-application": "similarity-checking"}], "deposited": {"date-parts": [[2026, 6, 12]], "date-time": "2026-06-12T15:35:06Z", "timestamp": 1781278506000}, "score": 24.605127, "resource": {"primary": {"URL": "https://www.techrxiv.org/doi/full/10.36227/techrxiv.175756679.96515147/v1"}}, "issued": {"date-parts": [[2025, 9, 11]]}, "references-count": 0, "URL": "https://doi.org/10.36227/techrxiv.175756679.96515147/v1", "relation": {"is-preprint-of": [{"id-type": "doi", "id": "10.1145/3787470.3787478", "asserted-by": "subject"}]}, "published": {"date-parts": [[2025, 9, 11]]}, "subtype": "preprint"}, {"institution": [{"name": "Research Square"}], "indexed": {"date-parts": [[2026, 3, 27]], "date-time": "2026-03-27T08:39:36Z", "timestamp": 1774600776660, "version": "3.50.1"}, "posted": {"date-parts": [[2026, 2, 18]]}, "group-title": "In Review", "reference-count": 0, "publisher": "Springer Science and Business Media LLC", "license": [{"start": {"date-parts": [[2026, 2, 18]], "date-time": "2026-02-18T00:00:00Z", "timestamp": 1771372800000}, "content-version": "unspecified", "delay-in-days": 0, "URL": "https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/"}], "content-domain": {"domain": [], "crossmark-restriction": false}, "accepted": {"date-parts": [[2026, 2, 13]]}, "abstract": "<title>Abstract</title>\n                <p>Agentic large language models (LLMs) increasingly rely on retrieved sources and tools, but their ability to reject these tools which undergo adversarial modification is uncertain. We evaluated 21 LLMs on 500 physician-validated emergency department and inpatient vignettes across 12 medical domains. For each vignette, models chose between an authentic guideline excerpt and a sham version with one adversarial modification, presented in random order (10,500 agentic decisions). Models selected the sham in 40.6% of evaluations (59.4% accuracy), with the highest failure rates for safety-critical changes including removed warnings, deleted allergy information, contraindication violations and dosing errors (54.2% to 61.7% failure). Choices were dominated by presentation bias: models favored the first option in 72.7% of decisions, shifting accuracy from 36.7% to 82.3% depending on sham position. Guideline selection in agentic systems is therefore vulnerable to poisoned sources and may require independent verification and ranking safeguards before clinical deployment. This finding is important especially in low-resource environments relying on AI agents as primary public health gatekeepers face disproportionate risks from poisoned tools</p>", "DOI": "10.21203/rs.3.rs-8872967/v1", "type": "posted-content", "created": {"date-parts": [[2026, 2, 18]], "date-time": "2026-02-18T08:53:16Z", "timestamp": 1771404796000}, "source": "Crossref", "is-referenced-by-count": 1, "title": ["When Agentic LLMs Trust Poisoned Tools: Vulnerability of Clinical LLMs to Adversarial Guidelines"], "prefix": "10.21203", "author": [{"ORCID": "https://orcid.org/0009-0001-0438-0827", "authenticated-orcid": false, "given": "Mahmud", "family": "Omar", "sequence": "first", "affiliation": [{"name": "Icahn School of Medicine at Mount Sinai"}], "role": [{"role": "author", "vocabulary": "crossref"}]}, {"ORCID": "https://orcid.org/0009-0000-7542-8608", "authenticated-orcid": false, "given": "Alon", "family": "Gorenshtien", "sequence": "additional", "affiliation": [{"name": "Icahn School of Medicine at Mount Sinai"}], "role": [{"role": "author", "vocabulary": "crossref"}]}, {"given": "Yiftach", "family": "Barash", "sequence": "additional", "affiliation": [{"name": "Division of Vascular and Interventional Radiology, Department of Radiology, Beth Israel Deaconess Medical Center, Harvard Medical School, Boston, MA, USA"}], "role": [{"role": "author", "vocabulary": "crossref"}]}, {"ORCID": "https://orcid.org/0000-0001-6319-4314", "authenticated-orcid": false, "given": "Girish", "family": "Nadkarni", "sequence": "additional", "affiliation": [{"name": "The Windreich Department of Artificial Intelligence and Human Health, Mount Sinai Medical Center, NY, USA"}], "role": [{"role": "author", "vocabulary": "crossref"}]}, {"given": "Eyal", "family": "Klang", "sequence": "additional", "affiliation": [{"name": "Icahn School of Medicine at Mount Sinai"}], "role": [{"role": "author", "vocabulary": "crossref"}]}], "member": "297", "link": [{"URL": "https://www.researchsquare.com/article/rs-8872967/v1", "content-type": "text/html", "content-version": "vor", "intended-application": "text-mining"}, {"URL": "https://www.researchsquare.com/article/rs-8872967/v1.html", "content-type": "unspecified", "content-version": "vor", "intended-application": "similarity-checking"}], "deposited": {"date-parts": [[2026, 3, 12]], "date-time": "2026-03-12T08:31:12Z", "timestamp": 1773304272000}, "score": 24.333015, "resource": {"primary": {"URL": "https://www.researchsquare.com/article/rs-8872967/v1"}}, "issued": {"date-parts": [[2026, 2, 18]]}, "references-count": 0, "URL": "https://doi.org/10.21203/rs.3.rs-8872967/v1", "published": {"date-parts": [[2026, 2, 18]]}, "subtype": "preprint"}], "items-per-page": 5, "query": {"start-index": 0, "search-terms": null}}}